  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $2$ is not prime or $2\\notin Q.$\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?",
  "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\u00ac(A\u2228B)=\u00acA\u2227\u00acB\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(2\\text{\u00a0is not prime}\\text{\u00a0or\u00a0}2\\notin Q) & \u2261\u00ac((2\\text{\u00a0is not prime})\u2228(2\\notin Q)) \\\\  & \u2261\u00ac(2\\text{\u00a0is not prime})\u2227\u00ac(2\\notin Q) \\\\  & \u2261(2\\text{\u00a0is prime})\u2227(2\\in Q) \\\\  & \u22612\\text{\u00a0is prime}\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}2\\in Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
